From b0c9c03753f48d7e968d349deabbbf0e02ecc3fe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work10_kasan_plan <adbtrr_work10_kasan_plan@local>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 15:57:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mm/kasan: collapse the entry points in the user pass of a
 split build

Linking libumm fails with KASan on:

  mm_free.o: in function `mm_free_internal':
  undefined reference to `kasan_poison'

os/mm is compiled twice in a protected build, into libkmm with __KERNEL__
defined and into libumm without it. os/mm/kasan/Make.defs builds the
runtime only for the first of those, deliberately, because the user heap
is never registered and carrying the entry points into the user image
would add several kilobytes for calls that could only ever find no region
and return.

The allocator hooks did not agree with that. mm_initialize.c guards its
kasan_register() call on CONFIG_BUILD_FLAT or __KERNEL__, but the six
poison and unpoison calls added alongside it were left unguarded, so the
user pass emitted real calls to a runtime that is not linked into it.
That is an undefined reference in every user image, and it surfaces at
the end of a full build rather than where the decision was made.

Guard the whole API in the header instead of at each call site: the entry
points are real in a flat build and in the kernel pass, and collapse to
the existing no-op stubs everywhere else. The condition is the negation of
the one mm_initialize.c already applies, so registration and the poison
calls now agree on which pass owns KASan, and the shared sources need no
conditional compilation of their own.

The stubs consume their arguments, so the locals the call sites keep just
to pass a size do not become set but unused in the user pass.

Verified by compiling the call site pattern in all four combinations and
counting undefined references: KASan off 0, flat build 6, protected
kernel pass 6, protected user pass 6 before this change and 0 after. A
link against an archive built without the runtime, which is what libumm
is, reproduces the reported error before and succeeds after. The self
test app, which is user space and includes this header for the case enum
only, is unaffected: the enum is declared outside the guard.
---
 os/include/tinyara/mm/kasan.h | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/os/include/tinyara/mm/kasan.h b/os/include/tinyara/mm/kasan.h
index 755ec1c8..15082a01 100644
--- a/os/include/tinyara/mm/kasan.h
+++ b/os/include/tinyara/mm/kasan.h
@@ -82,10 +82,28 @@ enum kasan_selftest_e {
  * Pre-processor Definitions
  ****************************************************************************/
 
-#ifndef CONFIG_MM_KASAN
+/* The entry points are real in two cases only: a flat build, where there is
+ * one image and one heap, and the kernel pass of a split build, which is the
+ * pass that compiles with __KERNEL__ defined.
+ *
+ * Everywhere else they collapse to nothing. The user pass of a split build
+ * compiles the same memory manager sources, but allocates from a heap that is
+ * never registered, and os/mm/kasan/Make.defs deliberately keeps the runtime
+ * out of libumm. Leaving the call sites as real calls there would put an
+ * undefined reference to kasan_poison into every user image, for a call that
+ * could only ever find no region and return.
+ *
+ * This is the same test mm_initialize.c applies before registering a heap,
+ * written the other way round, so registration and the poison calls agree on
+ * which pass owns KASan.
+ */
+
+#if !defined(CONFIG_MM_KASAN) || \
+	(!defined(CONFIG_BUILD_FLAT) && !defined(__KERNEL__))
 
-/* With KASan disabled every entry point collapses to nothing, so the call
- * sites in the memory manager need no conditional compilation of their own.
+/* With KASan disabled, or in the user pass, every entry point collapses to
+ * nothing, so the call sites in the memory manager need no conditional
+ * compilation of their own.
  *
  * Each stub still consumes its arguments. A call site typically keeps a size
  * in a local just to pass it here, and discarding the argument would leave
@@ -253,5 +271,5 @@ int kasan_selftest(int testcase);
 }
 #endif
 
-#endif							/* CONFIG_MM_KASAN */
+#endif							/* KASan real in this pass */
 #endif							/* __INCLUDE_MM_KASAN_H */
-- 
